window.__labLoaded({"id":"2.2","title":"Counting: How Many Ways?","goal":"Count podiums, committees and lottery tickets with factorials, permutations and combinations, and confirm the counts by listing the possibilities.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Factorials","text":"n! is the number of ways to line up n different things. Python's math module has factorial built in. By convention 0! equals 1.","code":"import math\n\nfor n in [0, 1, 4, 5, 8]:\n    print(f'{n}! = {math.factorial(n)}')","out":"0! = 1\n1! = 1\n4! = 24\n5! = 120\n8! = 40320","figs":[]},{"title":"Permutations and combinations","text":"math.perm(n, r) counts ordered selections (a podium of 3 from 8 runners). math.comb(n, r) counts unordered ones (a 3-person committee from 10). Dividing the permutations by r! gives the combinations.","code":"print('podiums, perm(8, 3)       =', math.perm(8, 3))\nprint('committees, comb(10, 3)   =', math.comb(10, 3))\nprint('perm(10, 3) / 3!          =', math.perm(10, 3) // math.factorial(3))\nprint('poker hands, comb(52, 5)  =', math.comb(52, 5))","out":"podiums, perm(8, 3)       = 336\ncommittees, comb(10, 3)   = 120\nperm(10, 3) / 3!          = 120\npoker hands, comb(52, 5)  = 2598960","figs":[]},{"title":"List them to confirm","text":"For small cases we can write out every selection with itertools and count it. Choosing 3 of 5 people gives 60 ordered lists but only 10 groups, a ratio of 3! = 6.","code":"from itertools import permutations, combinations\n\npeople = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E']\nordered = list(permutations(people, 3))\ngroups = list(combinations(people, 3))\n\nprint('ordered lists:', len(ordered), ' formula:', math.perm(5, 3))\nprint('groups:', len(groups), ' formula:', math.comb(5, 3))\nprint('first groups:', [''.join(g) for g in groups[:4]])","out":"ordered lists: 60  formula: 60\ngroups: 10  formula: 10\nfirst groups: ['ABC', 'ABD', 'ABE', 'ACD']","figs":[]},{"title":"From counting to probability","text":"One lottery ticket wins out of every possible set of 6 numbers from 49. The chance is 1 divided by the number of tickets. The all-hearts hand is (ways to pick 5 hearts) divided by (all hands).","code":"tickets = math.comb(49, 6)\nprint(f'tickets = {tickets:,}')\nprint(f'P(jackpot) = {1 / tickets:.2e}')\n\nhearts = math.comb(13, 5) / math.comb(52, 5)\nprint(f'P(5 hearts) = {math.comb(13, 5)} / {math.comb(52, 5):,} = {hearts:.4f}')","out":"tickets = 13,983,816\nP(jackpot) = 7.15e-08\nP(5 hearts) = 1287 / 2,598,960 = 0.0005","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"factorial(4)                 # 4!\nchoose(10, 3)                # committees\nchoose(8, 3) * factorial(3)  # podiums (permutations)\nchoose(52, 5)                # poker hands\nchoose(49, 6)                # lottery tickets\n1 / choose(49, 6)\nncol(combn(5, 3))            # lists the 10 groups\nchoose(13, 5) / choose(52, 5)","expect":"24; 120; 336; 2598960; 13983816; 7.151e-08; 10; 0.0005","note":"Base R has choose() and factorial() but no permutation function, so we multiply choose(n, r) by factorial(r). Python's value for 5 hearts is 0.0005 (0.000495)."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use the Arithmetic functions, LNGAMMA is in the list)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE fact4 = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(5))).\nCOMPUTE perm83 = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(9) - LNGAMMA(6))).\nCOMPUTE comb103 = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(11) - LNGAMMA(4) - LNGAMMA(8))).\nCOMPUTE lottery = RND(EXP(LNGAMMA(50) - LNGAMMA(7) - LNGAMMA(44))).\nFORMATS fact4 TO lottery (F10.0).\nLIST VARIABLES=fact4 perm83 comb103 lottery.","note":"SPSS has no built-in factorial or combination menu item. The closest honest route is LNGAMMA, because LNGAMMA(n + 1) is the log of n!. We turn it back with EXP and round, which gives 24, 336, 120 and 13983816."},"stata":{"code":"display factorial(4)\ndisplay comb(10, 3)\ndisplay comb(8, 3) * factorial(3)\ndisplay comb(52, 5)\ndisplay %12.0f comb(49, 6)\ndisplay 1/comb(49, 6)\ndisplay comb(13, 5)/comb(52, 5)","note":"Stata's comb(n, k) and factorial() match Python; there is no permutation function, so podiums are comb(8, 3) times 3!. The %12.0f format stops the big lottery number from showing in scientific notation."},"try":["How many 4-person committees can be chosen from 10 people? Predict whether it is more or fewer than 120, then run it.","Change the 3 in permutations(people, 3) to 2. Predict both counts, then check the formulas."]});
